## 2. The Rocket Equation Applied

Tsiolkovsky:
$$\Delta v = I_{sp} \cdot g_0 \cdot \ln\left(\frac{m_0}{m_f}\right)$$

**Assumed propulsion:**
- Primary: Methalox (Raptor-class), Isp = 380 s vacuum
- Upper stage / deep space: LH₂/LOX, Isp = 450 s vacuum

### 2.1 Ground → LEO Segment (methalox)

$$R = e^{6100 / (380 \times 9.81)} = e^{1.636} = 5.13$$

Propellant fraction = 1 − 1/R = **80.5%** of wet mass burned to reach LEO.
Dry-mass-plus-payload fraction = **19.5%** of wet mass.

Compare to conventional ground launch at 9.4 km/s:
$$R = e^{2.52} = 12.46 \quad \text{→ dry+payload fraction} = 8.0\%$$

**BGKPJR-VG gives 2.4× better mass fraction at LEO.** This is where the math gets serious.

### 2.2 LEO → Mars Surface (methalox, with LIDS refueling at depot)

After refueling at LEO fuel depot, fresh full tanks:

| Burn | Δv | Mass ratio R | Propellant fraction |
|---|---|---|---|
| TMI | 3.6 km/s | 2.63 | 61.9% |
| MOI (aerobrake) | 0.5 km/s | 1.14 | 12.3% |
| Descent | 0.8 km/s | 1.24 | 19.4% |

