## 3. Vehicle Sizing — Three Scenarios

Reference tunnel-exit wet mass: **500 tonnes** (between Falcon 9 and Starship class).

### Scenario A: Crewed Mars Mission (4g tunnel, 37 km)

| Stage | Wet Mass | Propellant Burned | End-of-Burn Mass |
|---|---|---|---|
| Tunnel exit | 500.0 t | — | 500.0 t |
| Arrive at LEO | — | 402.5 t | 97.5 t |
| **LEO refuel (LIDS dock)** | **500.0 t** | — | 500.0 t |
| After TMI | — | 309.5 t | 190.5 t |
| After MOI (aerobrake) | — | 23.4 t | 167.1 t |
| After Mars landing | — | 31.7 t | **135.4 t on Mars surface** |

Subtract crew-rated dry structure (life support, rad shielding, return fuel reserve): **~100 t**

**Usable Mars surface delivery: ~35 tonnes** (crew of 4–6 + supplies + habitat seed + return ascent vehicle margin)

### Scenario B: Robotic Cargo Mars Mission (10g tunnel, 14.5 km)

Same 500 t wet mass, but the tunnel is 60% cheaper to build (shorter + lighter crew-rating standards not required).

| Stage | End-of-Burn Mass |
|---|---|
| Arrive at LEO | 97.5 t |
| After LEO refuel | 500.0 t |
| After TMI + MOI + landing | 135.4 t on Mars surface |

Subtract robotic dry structure (no life support, minimal rad shielding): **~40 t**

**Usable Mars surface delivery: ~95 tonnes** (rovers, ISRU plants, habitat modules, fuel for return samples)

### Scenario C: Super-heavy Robotic (20g tunnel, 7.3 km) — cargo only

Short, cheap tunnel; brutal g-force. Robot & structural cargo only.

**Usable Mars surface delivery: ~105 tonnes per flight, infrastructure cost ~30% of crewed version.**

