## 4. G-Force Analysis — The Crew Rating Question

### 4.1 Tunnel Exit Velocity Math

For Mach 5 exit = 1,700 m/s:

| G-Load | Acceleration | Time in Tunnel | Tunnel Length |
|---|---|---|---|
| **3g** | 29.4 m/s² | **57.8 s** | **49.2 km** |
| **4g** | 39.2 m/s² | **43.4 s** | **36.9 km** |
| **6g** | 58.9 m/s² | 28.9 s | 24.5 km |
| **10g** | 98.1 m/s² | 17.3 s | 14.7 km |
| **20g** | 196.2 m/s² | 8.7 s | 7.4 km |

### 4.2 Human Tolerance Reference Points

| Scenario | G-Load | Duration | Outcome |
|---|---|---|---|
| Space Shuttle ascent | 3g | ~8 min | Standard crew, no training needed beyond basic |
| Virgin Galactic ascent | 3.5g sustained, 6g peak | ~30 s | Space tourism cleared |
| Soyuz ascent | 4g | ~9 min | Cosmonaut training required |
| Apollo reentry (ballistic) | 7g peak | ~10 s | Survivable but unpleasant |
| Fighter pilot with g-suit | 9g sustained | ~20 s | Elite pilots only |
| G-LOC threshold (no training) | 4–6g | 10+ s | Loss of consciousness |

### 4.3 Verdict Matrix

| Tunnel G-Load | Duration | Crew Feasibility |
|---|---|---|
| **3g** | 58 s | ✅ Civilian tourism compatible (Virgin Galactic class) |
| **4g** | 43 s | ✅ Standard astronaut training sufficient (Soyuz class) |
| **6g** | 29 s | ⚠️ Trained crew only, g-suits required |
| **10g** | 17 s | ❌ Robotic only — structural g-load limit for most hardware |
| **20g** | 9 s | ❌ Specially hardened cargo only — no avionics, no fluids |

### 4.4 The Direction-of-G Advantage

Tunnel g-force is **horizontal** (eyeballs-back, "Gx" axis) — humans tolerate this **far better** than vertical ("Gz" axis) g-force used in fighter maneuvering. Sustained 4g Gx is comparable to 2.5g Gz in physiological impact.

**This means our 4g tunnel is easier on crew than Shuttle launch was.**

