## 5. Maximum Safe Payload + Tare Combined

Payload mass is limited by two independent constraints — the lower of the two wins.

### 5.1 Propulsion Constraint (rocket equation)

For 500 t wet mass at tunnel exit: max delivered-to-Mars-surface mass = 135 t (as derived in §3).

### 5.2 Tunnel Structural Constraint (sled thrust capacity)

Maglev thrust capacity scales with magnetic field strength and coil current. At 4g with 500 t vehicle mass:

$$F = m \cdot a = 500{,}000 \text{ kg} \times 39.2 \text{ m/s}^2 = 1.96 \times 10^7 \text{ N (19.6 MN)}$$

For reference, the Saturn V produced 34 MN of thrust — so we need a maglev thrust capacity of about 58% of a Saturn V, sustained for 43 seconds. **Achievable with current superconducting magnet technology.**

### 5.3 G-Force-Limited Payload Table

| Vehicle Wet Mass | 4g Tunnel (Crewed) | 10g Tunnel (Robot) | 20g Tunnel (Cargo) |
|---|---|---|---|
| 250 t | 18 t Mars surface | 55 t | 60 t |
| **500 t** | **35 t Mars surface (4–6 crew)** | **95 t** | **105 t** |
| 1,000 t | 80 t Mars surface | 200 t | 220 t |
| 1,500 t (Starship-class) | 130 t Mars surface | 315 t | 345 t |

