## 6. Recommended Architecture: Dual-Tunnel Facility

Based on the numbers above, **one tunnel is the wrong answer.** Build two, sharing the launch site.

### 6.1 Facility Layout

- [ ] **Tunnel A — Crewed (37 km, 4g):** Manned Mars missions, space tourism, ISS-class deliveries
- [ ] **Tunnel B — Cargo (14.5 km, 10g):** Robotic precursors, fuel depot resupply, ISRU plant delivery, satellite constellations
- [ ] **Shared infrastructure:**
  - LH₂ cryogenic plant (one plant, two membrane gates)
  - Power grid (combined 300–400 MW demand)
  - Maglev control systems
  - Range safety & tracking
  - Mission control & support facilities

### 6.2 Cost Profile

| Element | Single Crewed Tunnel | Dual Tunnel | Savings |
|---|---|---|---|
| Tunnel A (37 km) | $45B | $45B | — |
| Tunnel B (14.5 km) | — | $18B | — |
| Shared infra (amortized) | $35B | $40B | vs. $70B if built separately |
| **Total** | **$80B** | **$103B** | **Saves $30B vs two standalone sites** |

### 6.3 Mission Architecture Benefit

- Cargo missions prepare Mars surface before humans arrive (ISRU, habitat, fuel production)
- Crewed missions arrive to a pre-provisioned site
- This mirrors the Mars Direct / Mars Semi-Direct architecture — BGKPJR-VG enables it at 40% the cost of conventional launch

